msgid "In <0>kool, kooli, kooli</0> the second and third forms are the same word. Many short words work this way, such as <1/>, town. In some words ending in a vowel all three match: <2/>, house, is <3>maja, maja, maja</3>."
msgstr "In <0>kool, kooli, kooli</0> the second and third forms are the same word. Many short words work this way, such as <1/>, town. In some words ending in a vowel all three match: <2/>, house, is <3>maja, maja, maja</3>."

msgid "In the second, <0>ee</0> shortens and <1>s</1> becomes <2>h</2>; the third ends in <3>-st</3>."
msgstr "In the second, <0>ee</0> shortens and <1>s</1> becomes <2>h</2>; the third ends in <3>-st</3>."

msgid "Its second and third forms are the same."
msgstr "Its second and third forms are the same."

msgid "land, country"
msgstr "land, country"

msgid "Learn the vowel at the end of the third form along with the word: <0>poodi, lauda, turgu</0>."
msgstr "Learn the vowel at the end of the third form along with the word: <0>poodi, lauda, turgu</0>."

msgid "Lesson {stems} showed letters dropping or changing before an ending: <0/> becomes <1/>. That change is the second form. In these words the third form has the first form’s letters back: <2/>."
msgstr "Lesson {stems} showed letters dropping or changing before an ending: <0/> becomes <1/>. That change is the second form. In these words the third form has the first form’s letters back: <2/>."

msgid "Like <0>küsimus</0>, <1>vastus</1> has a second form in <2>-use</2>: <3>vastuse</3>."
msgstr "Like <0>küsimus</0>, <1>vastus</1> has a second form in <2>-use</2>: <3>vastuse</3>."

msgid "Listening only, no microphone"
msgstr "Listening only, no microphone"

msgid "Look up <0/>, school, and a dictionary gives you three words: <1>kool, kooli, kooli</1>. Every noun is listed this way, in the same order. Most of its other forms are built on one of the three. Estonian calls them <2/>, the main forms. This course calls them the first, second and third form."
msgstr "Look up <0/>, school, and a dictionary gives you three words: <1>kool, kooli, kooli</1>. Every noun is listed this way, in the same order. Most of its other forms are built on one of the three. Estonian calls them <2/>, the main forms. This course calls them the first, second and third form."

